        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Gotchas",
          "text": "- Le menu in France is a fixed-price set of courses (entrée + plat + dessert). The list of all dishes is la carte. In English, “menu” means la carte: On pourrait avoir la carte ?\n- L'entrée = starter, le plat (principal) = main course. Do not translate “entrée” as “main course”: that's the American meaning.\n- Une formule is a cheaper set menu, often at lunchtime (formule du midi). L'ardoise (slate) is the blackboard with the dishes of the day.\n- On pourrait avoir… is a polite request (literally “we could have…”). Je voudrais… (I would like) is polite; Je veux… is too direct with a waiter.\n- L'addition = the bill. Ask for it with L'addition, s'il vous plaît.\n- Switzerland uses francs suisses (CHF); France and Belgium use euros. Prices are often written 12,50 € (comma for decimals).\n- Service is included in French prices (service compris). A tip is optional, a few coins for good service."
        },
        {
