    {
      "id": "passe-compose-avoir",
      "title": "Le passé composé avec avoir",
      "minutes": 9,
      "lede": "The passé composé is the main tense for telling what happened. It has two parts: avoir in the present + the past participle of the verb.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Réserver et finir",
          "headers": [
            "Sujet",
            "réserver",
            "finir"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "j'",
              "ai réservé",
              "ai fini"
            ],
            [
              "tu",
              "as réservé",
              "as fini"
            ],
            [
              "il / elle / on",
              "a réservé",
              "a fini"
            ],
            [
              "nous",
              "avons réservé",
              "avons fini"
            ],
            [
              "vous",
              "avez réservé",
              "avez fini"
            ],
            [
              "ils / elles",
              "ont réservé",
              "ont fini"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Former le participe passé",
          "headers": [
            "Infinitif",
            "Participe",
            "Exemple"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "verbes en -er",
              "-é",
              "réserver → réservé"
            ],
            [
              "verbes en -ir",
              "-i",
              "finir → fini"
            ],
            [
              "faire (irrégulier)",
              "fait",
              "faire → fait"
            ],
            [
              "prendre (irrégulier)",
              "pris",
              "prendre → pris"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Gotchas",
          "text": "- One French past tense covers three English ones: J'ai mangé = I ate, I have eaten, I did eat. French has no separate “have eaten”.\n- The auxiliary (avoir) is the one that changes with the subject; the participle stays the same: nous avons mangé, ils ont mangé. With avoir, the participle does not agree with the subject.\n- Parler, parlé, parlez, parler all sound the same [paʁle]. To know which spelling to use after avoir, replace the verb with vendre: you would say J'ai vendu (not J'ai vendre), so you write the participle: J'ai parlé.\n- Je becomes j' before ai: j'ai, not “je ai”. Time words that go with the passé composé: hier, ce matin, samedi dernier, il y a deux jours.\n- Questions: use intonation or est-ce que: Vous avez choisi ? Est-ce que vous avez réservé ? Short answer: Oui, j'ai réservé.\n- Irregular participles to learn by heart: faire → fait, prendre → pris, avoir → eu, être → été, voir → vu, boire → bu."
        },
