    {
      "id": "on-le-pronom",
      "title": "Le pronom on",
      "minutes": 5,
      "lede": "On is the everyday word for nous. It looks like a singular pronoun (il, elle), but it often means a group.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "On = nous",
          "headers": [
            "Avec nous",
            "Avec on"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Nous avons fini.",
              "On a fini."
            ],
            [
              "Nous allons au restaurant.",
              "On va au restaurant."
            ],
            [
              "Nous pouvons avoir la carte ?",
              "On peut avoir la carte ?"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Gotchas",
          "text": "- On is always followed by the il/elle form of the verb: on mange, on a mangé, on va, on fait. Never “on mangent”.\n- On = nous in conversation (On y va ?) but also “people / one / they” in general: En France, on dîne vers vingt heures.\n- Liaison: on a [ɔ̃na], on est [ɔ̃nɛ]: the n links to the vowel. In On pourrait avoir la carte ?, pourrait is the polite “could” form (conditional): you only need to know it as a set phrase.\n- In careful writing, nous is more formal. In an email to a boss, nous ; at a restaurant or with friends, on."
        },
        {
          "type": "phrases",
          "title": "Écoutez",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "On a réservé une table."
            },
            {
              "fr": "On pourrait avoir la carte ?"
            },
            {
              "fr": "On va au restaurant ce soir ?"
            },
            {
              "fr": "En France, on dîne tard."
            },
