      "id": "sons-e-in",
      "title": "Les sons [ɛ] et [ɛ̃]",
      "minutes": 4,
      "lede": "[ɛ] is oral (open e, as in « dessert »), [ɛ̃] is nasal (as in « vin »): the air also passes through the nose.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Oral ou nasal ?",
          "headers": [
            "Son",
            "Exemples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "[ɛ] oral",
              "une entrée · un dessert · la fraise"
            ],
            [
              "[ɛ̃] nasal",
              "le vin · vingt · un · bien · je reviens"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "How to hear it",
          "text": "- The nasal [ɛ̃] is spelled in, im, ain, aim, ein, ien (bien) and un. The n or m is not pronounced as a consonant.\n- Many speakers now say un [œ̃] exactly like [ɛ̃]. Compare un [œ̃] with une [yn]: the second has a real n.\n- When n is followed by a vowel, the vowel is no longer nasal: vin [vɛ̃] / vigne, bien [bjɛ̃] / bienvenue [bjɛ̃vny], voisin [vwazɛ̃] / voisine [vwazin]. The feminine has a pronounced n."
        },
